(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_respirationinplants=[{id:"neet-2026r-q105",pn:105,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Glycolysis products",q:"How many molecules of pyruvic acid are produced at the end of glycolysis from 206 molecules of glucose?",o:["309","103","412","206"],c:2,s:`One glucose molecule gives two molecules of pyruvic acid at the end of glycolysis.
So 206 glucose molecules give 206 \xD7 2 = 412 molecules of pyruvic acid.`},{id:"neet-2026-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Respiratory quotient",q:`2(C\u2085\u2081H\u2089\u2088O\u2086) + 145O\u2082 \u2192 102CO\u2082 + 98H\u2082O + energy
The Respiratory Quotient (RQ) of a biomolecule used for respiration, as per the above equation would be:`,o:["Less than 0.5","Between 0.5 and 0.95","Between 1.25 and 2","1.0"],c:1,s:`RQ = (Volume of CO\u2082 evolved)/(Volume of O\u2082 consumed).
From the equation, CO\u2082 evolved = 102 and O\u2082 consumed = 145.
RQ = 102/145 = 0.7.
This value lies between 0.5 and 0.95.
Note: The paper prints C\u2086 in this formula; C\u2085\u2081H\u2089\u2088O\u2086 (a triglyceride) is meant.`},{id:"neet-2026-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Site of respiratory steps",q:`Match List-I with List-II :
List-I (Process) \u2014 List-II (Location)
A. Glycolysis \u2014 I. Inner mitochondrial membrane
B. ETS \u2014 II. Mitochondrial matrix
C. Accumulation of protons \u2014 III. Cytoplasm
D. Krebs' cycle \u2014 IV. Intermembrane space`,o:["A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],c:1,s:`Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm in all living organisms.
The electron transport system is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane.
Protons accumulate in the intermembrane space.
Krebs' cycle takes place in the mitochondrial matrix.`},{id:"neet-2025-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Electron transport chain complexes",q:"The complex II of mitochondrial electron transport chain is also known as",o:["Cytochrome bc\u2081","Succinate dehydrogenase","Cytochrome c oxidase","NADH dehydrogenase"],c:1,s:`Complex II of the mitochondrial electron transport chain is succinate dehydrogenase.
Cytochrome c oxidase is complex IV.
NADH dehydrogenase is complex I.
Cytochrome bc\u2081 is complex III.`},{id:"neet-2024-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Krebs cycle",q:"Identify the step in tricarboxylic acid cycle, which does not involve oxidation of substrate.",o:["Succinyl-CoA \u2192 Succinic acid","Isocitrate \u2192 \u03B1-ketoglutaric acid","Malic acid \u2192 Oxaloacetic acid","Succinic acid \u2192 Malic acid"],c:0,s:`Oxidation is loss of electrons (often as hydrogen) from a molecule, so its oxidation state rises.
The electrons go to an acceptor, which is reduced.
Conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid does not involve oxidation of the substrate.`},{id:"neet-2024-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Site of respiration steps",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List I \u2014 List II
A. Citric acid cycle \u2014 I. Cytoplasm
B. Glycolysis \u2014 II. Mitochondrial matrix
C. Electron transport system \u2014 III. Intermembrane space of mitochondria
D. Proton gradient \u2014 IV. Inner mitochondrial membrane.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`The citric acid cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix.
Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol in most organisms.
The electron transport system is in the inner mitochondrial membrane.
The proton gradient is built up in the intermembrane space.
So A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2023-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Glycolysis ATP use",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : ATP is used at two steps in glycolysis.
Reason R : First ATP is used in converting glucose into glucose-6-phosphate and second ATP is used in conversion of fructose-6-phosphate into fructose-1-6-diphosphate.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],c:1,s:`ATP is used in two steps of glycolysis.
Step 1: glucose \u2192 glucose-6-phosphate.
Step 2: fructose-6-phosphate \u2192 fructose-1,6-bisphosphate.
ATP is used to phosphorylate the substrates, so R correctly explains A.`},{id:"neet-2023-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Stages of respiration",q:`Match List I with List II :
List I: A. Oxidative decarboxylation; B. Glycolysis; C. Oxidative phosphorylation; D. Tricarboxylic acid cycle
List II: I. Citrate synthase; II. Pyruvate dehydrogenase; III. Electron transport system; IV. EMP pathway
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A \u2013 II, B \u2013 IV, C \u2013 III, D \u2013 I","A \u2013 III, B \u2013 IV, C \u2013 II, D \u2013 I","A \u2013 II, B \u2013 IV, C \u2013 I, D \u2013 III","A \u2013 III, B \u2013 I, C \u2013 II, D \u2013 IV"],c:0,s:`Pyruvate undergoes oxidative decarboxylation in the mitochondrial matrix by pyruvate dehydrogenase: A-II.
Glycolysis is the EMP pathway: B-IV.
The electron transport system makes the proton gradient for phosphorylation, so it is oxidative phosphorylation: C-III.
The TCA cycle starts when citrate synthase joins acetyl group with OAA: D-I.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q106",pn:106,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"TCA cycle intermediates",q:"The 5-C compound formed during TCA cycle is:",o:["Fumaric acid","\u03B1-ketoglutaric acid","Oxalo succinic acid","Succinic acid"],c:1,s:`\u03B1-ketoglutaric acid has 5 carbons.
Fumaric acid has 4 carbons.
Oxalosuccinic acid has 6 carbons.
Succinic acid has 4 carbons.`},{id:"neet-2022-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Fermentation energy",q:"What amount of energy is released from glucose during lactic acid fermentation ?",o:["More than 18%","About 10%","Less than 7%","Approximately 15%"],c:2,s:`Less than 7% of the energy in glucose is released in lactic acid fermentation.
Not all of it is trapped as ATP bonds.`},{id:"neet-2022-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Glycolysis ATP yield",q:"What is the net gain of ATP when each molecule of glucose is converted to two molecules of pyruvic acid?",o:["Six","Two","Eight","Four"],c:1,s:`Glycolysis makes 4 ATP from one glucose.
2 ATP are used up in the process.
Net gain = 4 - 2 = 2 ATP.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q131",pn:131,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Krebs cycle decarboxylation",q:"The number of time(s) decarboxylation of isocitrate occurs during single TCA cycle is:",o:["Four","One","Two","Three"],c:1,s:`The TCA cycle has two decarboxylation steps.
In step 4 isocitrate loses H atoms and CO\u2082 and becomes the 5-carbon \u03B1-ketoglutarate.
In step 5 \u03B1-ketoglutarate reacts with coenzyme A and gives CO\u2082 and the 4-carbon succinyl-CoA.
The question asks only about isocitrate, so the answer is one.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q132",pn:132,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Amphibolic pathway and related terms",q:`Match List - I with List - II:
List - I: (a) Porins (b) leg haemoglobin (c) H\u207A accumulation (d) Respiration
List - II: (i) Pink coloured nodules (ii) Lumen of thylakoid (iii) Amphibolic pathway (iv) Huge pores in outer membrane of mitochondria
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],c:2,s:`Porins are proteins that make large pores in the outer membrane of mitochondria, plastids and bacteria.
Leghaemoglobin is the pink pigment of legume root nodules and mops up O\u2082 for N\u2082 fixation.
H\u207A collects in the thylakoid lumen.
Respiration is amphibolic because it has both breakdown and build-up roles.`},{id:"neet-2021-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Electron transport chain",q:"Which of the following statements of incorrect?",o:["Oxidation-reducation reactions produce proton gradient in respiration","During aerobic respiration, role of oxygen is limited to the terminal stage","In ETC (Electron Transport Chain), one molecule of NADH + H\u207A gives rise to 2 ATP molecules, and one FADH\u2082 gives rise to 3 ATP molecules","ATP is synthesized through complex V"],c:2,s:`The NCERT values are 3 ATP per NADH and 2 ATP per FADH\u2082, so statement 3 has them swapped and is incorrect.
Redox reactions build the proton gradient, O\u2082 is the final electron acceptor, and ATP synthase is complex V, so those statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2020-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Krebs cycle",q:"The number of substrate level phosphorylations in one turn of citric acid cycle is :",o:["One","Two","Three","Zero"],c:0,s:`In one turn of the citric acid cycle, succinyl-CoA to succinate forms one GTP/ATP by substrate level phosphorylation.
So the number is one.`},{id:"neet-2019-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Respiratory quotient",q:"Respiratory Quotient (RQ) value of tripalmitin is :",o:["0.07","0.09","0.9","0.7"],c:3,s:`Tripalmitin is a fat.
Fats need more oxygen than the CO2 they give out.
The RQ of fats is about 0.7.`},{id:"neet-2019-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Glycolysis",q:"Conversion of glucose to glucose-6phosphate, the first irreversible reaction of glycolysis, is catalyzed by",o:["Enolase","Phosphofructokinase","Aldolase","Hexokinase"],c:3,s:`In glycolysis, glucose gets a phosphate from ATP and becomes glucose-6-phosphate.
The enzyme for this step is hexokinase.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`}]})();
